1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,506
You are in charge of the

2
00:00:01,506 --> 00:00:03,254
most frightening thing aboard.

3
00:00:03,254 --> 00:00:04,308
Two small cannons.

4
00:00:04,308 --> 00:00:06,134
And the whole point of your

5
00:00:06,134 --> 00:00:08,425
job is to never have to use them.

6
00:00:08,425 --> 00:00:10,058
So far in this series,

7
00:00:10,058 --> 00:00:12,847
you have been Tom, the captain,

8
00:00:12,847 --> 00:00:18,285
the quartermaster, the surgeon, the carpenter and the navigator.

9
00:00:18,285 --> 00:00:20,877
This time, you are the man who

10
00:00:20,877 --> 00:00:23,720
sleeps nearest the gunpowder: the gunner.

11
00:00:24,238 --> 00:00:27,542
Imagine you are a gunner of about forty-five,

12
00:00:27,542 --> 00:00:29,382
aboard the same modest

13
00:00:29,382 --> 00:00:31,982
Caribbean sloop around seventeen seventeen.

14
00:00:32,681 --> 00:00:34,323
You are short and stocky,

15
00:00:34,323 --> 00:00:37,826
with a shaved head, a thick black beard going gray,

16
00:00:37,826 --> 00:00:40,211
a sleeveless leather jerkin over

17
00:00:40,211 --> 00:00:43,101
a gray shirt, a powder horn on a cord

18
00:00:43,101 --> 00:00:46,126
and a coil of slow match at your belt.

19
00:00:46,126 --> 00:00:47,691
You served on merchant ships

20
00:00:47,691 --> 00:00:50,220
before this one, and you came to

21
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:52,361
the pirates of your own free will.

22
00:00:52,816 --> 00:00:54,768
Years beside the guns have left you

23
00:00:54,768 --> 00:00:57,459
hearing about half of what the crew says.

24
00:00:57,459 --> 00:00:59,407
You consider it the better half.

25
00:00:59,878 --> 00:01:00,242
You,

26
00:01:00,242 --> 00:01:03,452
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

27
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:06,106
The problems come from trial records,

28
00:01:06,106 --> 00:01:07,678
eyewitness accounts,

29
00:01:07,678 --> 00:01:11,934
archaeology and historians' research on real pirate crews.

30
00:01:11,934 --> 00:01:14,446
And remember what the job was.

31
00:01:14,981 --> 00:01:17,457
Every gun you load exists to frighten,

32
00:01:17,457 --> 00:01:18,539
or to hurt,

33
00:01:18,897 --> 00:01:22,166
people who were only trying to do their work at sea.

34
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:24,472
The sailors aboard the ships you

35
00:01:24,472 --> 00:01:25,892
threaten are not scenery,

36
00:01:25,892 --> 00:01:29,081
even when our story keeps the violence off screen.

37
00:01:29,081 --> 00:01:30,896
Pirate crews valued gunners.

38
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:32,911
In the articles that Captain

39
00:01:32,911 --> 00:01:36,513
Charles Johnson printed for Bartholomew Roberts's crew,

40
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:37,881
the gunner received

41
00:01:37,881 --> 00:01:39,385
one and a half shares of a prize,

42
00:01:39,385 --> 00:01:42,162
the same as the master and the boatswain.

43
00:01:42,162 --> 00:01:43,522
The economist Peter Leeson

44
00:01:43,522 --> 00:01:45,529
reproduces the same clause.

45
00:01:45,529 --> 00:01:48,641
Johnson is a pen name, and his book of

46
00:01:48,641 --> 00:01:51,005
seventeen twenty-four mixes fact with invention,

47
00:01:51,005 --> 00:01:53,481
but on this point the two agree.

48
00:01:53,481 --> 00:01:55,473
Your day is mostly housekeeping.

49
00:01:55,473 --> 00:01:56,971
You check the two guns,

50
00:01:56,971 --> 00:01:58,915
their carriages and their ropes.

51
00:01:58,915 --> 00:02:00,499
You keep the powder dry,

52
00:02:00,499 --> 00:02:03,162
the shot stacked and the match coiled.

53
00:02:03,162 --> 00:02:05,548
You inspect every pistol aboard,

54
00:02:05,548 --> 00:02:07,725
because Roberts's articles,

55
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:09,260
as Johnson printed them,

56
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:11,723
required every man to keep his piece,

57
00:02:11,723 --> 00:02:15,141
pistols and cutlass clean and fit for service.

58
00:02:15,756 --> 00:02:18,905
You also run the most important rule aboard:

59
00:02:18,905 --> 00:02:20,742
no fire near the powder.

60
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:22,474
John Phillips's articles,

61
00:02:22,474 --> 00:02:24,276
which only Johnson prints,

62
00:02:24,276 --> 00:02:27,776
banned smoking in the hold without a cap on the pipe,

63
00:02:27,776 --> 00:02:30,169
and any candle without a lantern.

64
00:02:30,169 --> 00:02:32,842
A wooden ship full of powder,

65
00:02:32,842 --> 00:02:35,947
rum and tired men is not forgiving.

66
00:02:35,947 --> 00:02:37,235
Tom asks if he can

67
00:02:37,235 --> 00:02:39,577
light his pipe near the guns.

68
00:02:39,577 --> 00:02:41,360
You do not answer.

69
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:44,400
You just look at him until he goes away.

70
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,487
That is about half of gunnery.

71
00:02:46,487 --> 00:02:47,870
The other half is a

72
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:49,876
routine you could do asleep.

73
00:02:49,876 --> 00:02:51,490
Sponge out the barrel,

74
00:02:51,490 --> 00:02:54,776
so no spark survives from the last shot.

75
00:02:54,776 --> 00:02:56,118
Powder charge in,

76
00:02:56,118 --> 00:02:58,207
then a wad of old rope,

77
00:02:58,549 --> 00:03:02,273
then the ball, then another wad to hold it.

78
00:03:02,273 --> 00:03:04,443
Run the gun out through its port.

79
00:03:04,443 --> 00:03:07,159
Prick the cartridge through the touch-hole,

80
00:03:07,159 --> 00:03:08,982
prime it with fine powder,

81
00:03:08,982 --> 00:03:10,734
stand clear of the wheels,

82
00:03:10,734 --> 00:03:13,505
and touch it off with the glowing match.

83
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,674
Then do it all again,

84
00:03:15,674 --> 00:03:18,731
faster, while somebody is shooting back.

85
00:03:18,731 --> 00:03:20,514
You offer to teach Tom.

86
00:03:20,514 --> 00:03:22,797
The crew votes against it.

87
00:03:22,797 --> 00:03:24,036
Powder costs money,

88
00:03:24,036 --> 00:03:26,719
practice shots hit nothing worth selling,

89
00:03:26,719 --> 00:03:28,817
and nobody wants to explain to the

90
00:03:28,817 --> 00:03:31,139
quartermaster where the ball went.

91
00:03:31,139 --> 00:03:33,776
So Tom learns to sponge the barrel,

92
00:03:33,776 --> 00:03:35,471
and is very proud of it.

93
00:03:35,471 --> 00:03:38,726
It is, you admit, the most important step.

94
00:03:38,726 --> 00:03:40,003
Here is the secret

95
00:03:40,003 --> 00:03:42,023
nobody puts on the poster.

96
00:03:42,023 --> 00:03:43,403
On a pirate sloop,

97
00:03:43,403 --> 00:03:46,303
a good gunner almost never fires.

98
00:03:46,303 --> 00:03:49,026
Pirates wanted prizes, not battles.

99
00:03:49,543 --> 00:03:51,506
Leeson argues that crews promised

100
00:03:51,506 --> 00:03:53,229
mercy to merchant ships that

101
00:03:53,229 --> 00:03:54,830
surrendered without a fight,

102
00:03:54,830 --> 00:03:58,034
and threatened much worse for those that resisted.

103
00:03:58,034 --> 00:04:00,425
The legal scholar Magne Frostad

104
00:04:00,425 --> 00:04:03,116
told ScienceNorway that pirates hoped

105
00:04:03,116 --> 00:04:04,555
their flag alone would be

106
00:04:04,555 --> 00:04:07,370
frightening enough to make victims surrender.

107
00:04:07,370 --> 00:04:09,222
A fight risked wounded men,

108
00:04:09,222 --> 00:04:12,450
a damaged ship and a damaged prize.

109
00:04:12,985 --> 00:04:15,218
Every shot you fire into a merchant

110
00:04:15,218 --> 00:04:17,962
ship is a hole the carpenter has to fix later,

111
00:04:17,962 --> 00:04:20,096
in a ship you were planning to rob.

112
00:04:20,096 --> 00:04:21,863
So when a sail appears,

113
00:04:21,863 --> 00:04:25,562
and the chase begins, your moment is small.

114
00:04:25,562 --> 00:04:28,412
You fire one gun, aimed well ahead of

115
00:04:28,412 --> 00:04:30,759
the merchant's bow, to tell her to stop.

116
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:32,739
That was a real tactic.

117
00:04:32,739 --> 00:04:35,938
At the Cape Coast Castle trial in seventeen twenty-two,

118
00:04:35,938 --> 00:04:38,060
a Dutch captain said that

119
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:40,865
two pirate ships "fired and brought him

120
00:04:40,865 --> 00:04:43,631
to," which means they forced him to stop.

121
00:04:43,631 --> 00:04:46,133
For one bad moment, the merchant answers.

122
00:04:46,490 --> 00:04:48,598
A puff of smoke from her stern,

123
00:04:48,598 --> 00:04:50,834
a bang, and a ball skips across

124
00:04:50,834 --> 00:04:53,090
the water well short of your bow.

125
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:54,608
Her captain is brave,

126
00:04:54,608 --> 00:04:56,289
or frightened, or both.

127
00:04:56,289 --> 00:04:58,199
The crew looks at you.

128
00:04:58,684 --> 00:05:01,071
You keep your second gun quiet and wait,

129
00:05:01,071 --> 00:05:03,038
because a fight is exactly what

130
00:05:03,038 --> 00:05:06,295
nobody aboard either ship actually wants.

131
00:05:06,295 --> 00:05:08,097
The merchant lowers her sails.

132
00:05:08,097 --> 00:05:10,200
Nobody on either deck is hurt.

133
00:05:10,605 --> 00:05:12,025
The crew cheers the captain.

134
00:05:12,025 --> 00:05:14,333
You swab out the barrel and say nothing.

135
00:05:14,706 --> 00:05:16,233
A shot that did not need a

136
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:19,016
second shot is the best shot there is.

137
00:05:19,016 --> 00:05:20,225
Later, on her deck,

138
00:05:20,225 --> 00:05:23,016
you meet the man who fired that shot:

139
00:05:23,016 --> 00:05:26,405
the merchant's own gunner, gray-haired and shaking.

140
00:05:26,891 --> 00:05:28,327
He tells you his owners would

141
00:05:28,327 --> 00:05:30,446
have asked why he never fired at all.

142
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:32,690
You tell him it was a good shot,

143
00:05:32,690 --> 00:05:33,341
just short,

144
00:05:33,341 --> 00:05:36,051
and that short was the smartest place to put it.

145
00:05:36,051 --> 00:05:37,860
If he had hit your sloop,

146
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,971
your crew would not have been so calm.

147
00:05:39,971 --> 00:05:42,105
He looks at you as if he does

148
00:05:42,105 --> 00:05:43,847
not know whether to thank you.

149
00:05:43,847 --> 00:05:44,747
Neither do you.

150
00:05:44,747 --> 00:05:46,487
Then you go down into her hold,

151
00:05:46,487 --> 00:05:47,439
with the boarders,

152
00:05:47,439 --> 00:05:49,842
to collect your share of the shopping list.

153
00:05:49,842 --> 00:05:51,237
On Roberts's ships,

154
00:05:51,237 --> 00:05:55,459
a gunner went straight for the prize's gunner's stores:

155
00:05:55,459 --> 00:05:58,801
powder, shot, match, flints.

156
00:05:58,801 --> 00:06:01,958
At Cape Coast, two Dutch captains told

157
00:06:01,958 --> 00:06:04,407
the court that one of Roberts's men,

158
00:06:04,407 --> 00:06:06,631
Thomas Sutton, had come aboard their

159
00:06:06,631 --> 00:06:09,773
ships armed and taken their gunner's stores.

160
00:06:09,773 --> 00:06:11,094
The court record later

161
00:06:11,094 --> 00:06:12,692
lists Sutton as a gunner.

162
00:06:12,692 --> 00:06:15,496
In Johnson's version of the trial,

163
00:06:15,496 --> 00:06:17,724
one of the Dutch captains said Sutton had

164
00:06:17,724 --> 00:06:20,055
ordered all their gunner's stores out.

165
00:06:20,055 --> 00:06:22,844
Sutton interrupted him from the dock.

166
00:06:23,283 --> 00:06:25,125
The man was lying, he said.

167
00:06:25,125 --> 00:06:26,938
He had not taken half.

168
00:06:27,537 --> 00:06:29,298
You take powder in its barrels,

169
00:06:29,298 --> 00:06:30,673
a few bags of small shot

170
00:06:30,673 --> 00:06:32,852
and all the flints you can find.

171
00:06:32,852 --> 00:06:35,201
You leave the merchant one small gun and

172
00:06:35,201 --> 00:06:37,378
enough powder to fire a warning of his own,

173
00:06:37,378 --> 00:06:39,894
in case he meets somebody worse than you.

174
00:06:39,894 --> 00:06:42,858
There is always somebody worse than you.

175
00:06:42,858 --> 00:06:44,079
Gunners themselves

176
00:06:44,079 --> 00:06:46,114
were often taken by force.

177
00:06:46,633 --> 00:06:48,631
The court heard about one gunner taken

178
00:06:48,631 --> 00:06:50,746
from a Liverpool ship in seventeen twenty-one,

179
00:06:50,746 --> 00:06:52,955
whose own captain had begged the

180
00:06:52,955 --> 00:06:55,045
pirates to let him stay,

181
00:06:55,045 --> 00:06:58,758
"because he was his gunner, and very much wanted by him."

182
00:06:58,758 --> 00:07:00,391
The pirates refused.

183
00:07:00,391 --> 00:07:02,546
Witnesses said that same gunner

184
00:07:02,546 --> 00:07:04,513
had tried to escape several times.

185
00:07:04,513 --> 00:07:06,093
Once he slipped back aboard

186
00:07:06,093 --> 00:07:07,282
his own ship and hid,

187
00:07:07,282 --> 00:07:09,425
and the pirates sent for him by name.

188
00:07:09,425 --> 00:07:10,953
The court acquitted him.

189
00:07:10,953 --> 00:07:12,970
Another witness said the gunner of

190
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:14,672
the Bristol galley Mercy had been

191
00:07:14,672 --> 00:07:16,675
forced out of his ship with Roberts

192
00:07:16,675 --> 00:07:19,366
himself beating him aboard the pirate ship.

193
00:07:19,366 --> 00:07:21,458
Being good with a gun was not

194
00:07:21,458 --> 00:07:23,150
something you could hide for long.

195
00:07:23,150 --> 00:07:24,374
Others were simply put

196
00:07:24,374 --> 00:07:25,881
to work near the powder.

197
00:07:25,881 --> 00:07:27,776
A witness said that one man,

198
00:07:27,776 --> 00:07:30,869
Hugh Menzies, was forced off his ship and

199
00:07:30,869 --> 00:07:33,634
beaten twice because he cried and begged

200
00:07:33,634 --> 00:07:36,200
to get away, and that the pirates

201
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:38,947
mockingly called him "Dead Hugh Menzies."

202
00:07:39,546 --> 00:07:41,092
In the fight with the Swallow,

203
00:07:41,092 --> 00:07:42,936
he was stationed in the powder room,

204
00:07:42,936 --> 00:07:45,279
and the same witness said he did much

205
00:07:45,279 --> 00:07:47,670
to save the ship from being blown up.

206
00:07:47,670 --> 00:07:48,884
Another prisoner,

207
00:07:48,884 --> 00:07:52,821
a witness said, became gunner's mate only because Roberts's

208
00:07:52,821 --> 00:07:55,323
gunner swore that he should accept it.

209
00:07:55,842 --> 00:07:56,842
On a pirate ship,

210
00:07:56,842 --> 00:07:59,143
even a promotion could be an order.

211
00:07:59,709 --> 00:08:00,981
Back on your sloop,

212
00:08:00,981 --> 00:08:02,826
the new powder needs a home.

213
00:08:02,826 --> 00:08:05,514
You stow the barrels deep in the hold,

214
00:08:05,514 --> 00:08:06,698
in the driest corner,

215
00:08:06,698 --> 00:08:10,102
as far from the galley fire as a small sloop allows,

216
00:08:10,102 --> 00:08:12,038
which is not very far.

217
00:08:12,444 --> 00:08:14,046
You sleep on top of them.

218
00:08:14,046 --> 00:08:16,256
The crew considers this brave.

219
00:08:16,630 --> 00:08:18,574
You consider it supervision.

220
00:08:18,574 --> 00:08:21,946
We know what a real pirate gunner kept loaded,

221
00:08:21,946 --> 00:08:23,062
because Blackbeard's

222
00:08:23,062 --> 00:08:25,866
flagship sank with her guns still aboard.

223
00:08:25,866 --> 00:08:28,406
When the Queen Anne's Revenge ran aground

224
00:08:28,406 --> 00:08:30,143
at Beaufort Inlet,

225
00:08:30,143 --> 00:08:35,814
North Carolina, in June seventeen eighteen, she went down armed.

226
00:08:35,814 --> 00:08:38,344
A nineteen ninety-nine report from the

227
00:08:38,344 --> 00:08:40,628
North Carolina conservation laboratory

228
00:08:40,628 --> 00:08:43,785
explains that La Concorde had carried

229
00:08:43,785 --> 00:08:47,171
fourteen to sixteen guns as a French slave ship.

230
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:48,744
Blackbeard's crew

231
00:08:48,744 --> 00:08:50,957
increased that to as many as forty.

232
00:08:51,607 --> 00:08:54,255
Royal Museums Greenwich also says

233
00:08:54,255 --> 00:08:57,413
Blackbeard equipped her with forty guns.

234
00:08:57,413 --> 00:08:59,819
Archaeologists have found the guns.

235
00:09:00,194 --> 00:09:01,593
The nineteen ninety-nine report

236
00:09:01,593 --> 00:09:05,278
counted twenty-one cannons on the site so far.

237
00:09:05,278 --> 00:09:06,185
By two thousand thirteen,

238
00:09:06,185 --> 00:09:09,401
the Maritime Executive reported,

239
00:09:09,401 --> 00:09:12,161
twenty-nine had been located and twenty raised.

240
00:09:12,551 --> 00:09:15,938
Both describe most of them as cast-iron six-pounders,

241
00:09:15,938 --> 00:09:17,236
about eight feet long.

242
00:09:17,236 --> 00:09:19,176
Some were still loaded.

243
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:20,242
In two thousand eleven,

244
00:09:20,242 --> 00:09:22,779
the project's chief conservator,

245
00:09:22,779 --> 00:09:25,831
Sarah Watkins-Kenney, said that four cannons had

246
00:09:25,831 --> 00:09:27,581
been found loaded,

247
00:09:27,581 --> 00:09:31,109
"with cannon shot and wads in place ready to be fired."

248
00:09:31,499 --> 00:09:32,797
The nineteen ninety-nine report

249
00:09:32,797 --> 00:09:35,124
describes one small gun,

250
00:09:35,434 --> 00:09:38,604
cast in Sweden and dated seventeen thirteen.

251
00:09:39,208 --> 00:09:41,477
Inside, behind its wooden plug,

252
00:09:41,477 --> 00:09:43,915
conservators found a wad of rope,

253
00:09:43,915 --> 00:09:45,607
then three iron drift pins,

254
00:09:45,607 --> 00:09:49,024
which are heavy spikes used in shipbuilding,

255
00:09:49,024 --> 00:09:52,796
then a second wad, a solid round shot,

256
00:09:52,796 --> 00:09:56,589
a third wad and finally the powder charge.

257
00:09:56,589 --> 00:09:58,050
Somebody had loaded spare

258
00:09:58,050 --> 00:10:00,108
iron along with the ball.

259
00:10:00,108 --> 00:10:01,690
Even three centuries later,

260
00:10:01,690 --> 00:10:04,457
the report treats that powder as live.

261
00:10:04,457 --> 00:10:06,363
The conservators pulled the wads out

262
00:10:06,363 --> 00:10:09,156
with a copper wire hook, made a scoop from

263
00:10:09,156 --> 00:10:12,057
plastic pipe shaped like an old powder scoop,

264
00:10:12,057 --> 00:10:13,665
and filled the barrel with fresh

265
00:10:13,665 --> 00:10:16,369
water in case a metal tool struck a spark.

266
00:10:16,369 --> 00:10:18,188
A gunner would have approved.

267
00:10:18,188 --> 00:10:20,386
The same report describes two

268
00:10:20,386 --> 00:10:22,346
cast-iron hand grenades from the wreck,

269
00:10:22,346 --> 00:10:24,465
hollow spheres packed with gunpowder,

270
00:10:24,465 --> 00:10:26,052
with a wooden fuse.

271
00:10:26,052 --> 00:10:27,501
And in twenty eighteen,

272
00:10:27,501 --> 00:10:29,932
conservators found sixteen scraps of paper

273
00:10:29,932 --> 00:10:31,898
wedged inside the chamber of a

274
00:10:31,898 --> 00:10:34,182
breech-loading cannon from the wreck.

275
00:10:34,182 --> 00:10:36,354
The Associated Press reported that

276
00:10:36,354 --> 00:10:39,022
the fabric and plug around them had

277
00:10:39,022 --> 00:10:41,354
protected the paper for three hundred years.

278
00:10:41,354 --> 00:10:43,828
Researchers identified the scraps.

279
00:10:43,828 --> 00:10:46,210
They came from a first edition,

280
00:10:46,210 --> 00:10:47,761
printed in seventeen twelve,

281
00:10:48,070 --> 00:10:51,563
of Captain Edward Cooke's A Voyage to the South Sea,

282
00:10:52,065 --> 00:10:55,208
the story of a privateering voyage around the world.

283
00:10:55,208 --> 00:10:57,245
Cooke had sailed on that

284
00:10:57,245 --> 00:10:59,304
voyage under Woodes Rogers.

285
00:10:59,304 --> 00:11:01,310
Yes, that Woodes Rogers.

286
00:11:01,310 --> 00:11:04,444
The same man who arrived at Nassau as

287
00:11:04,444 --> 00:11:06,443
governor in seventeen eighteen,

288
00:11:06,443 --> 00:11:09,231
with the king's pardon and warships behind him.

289
00:11:09,733 --> 00:11:11,618
Blackbeard's gunners may have used a

290
00:11:11,618 --> 00:11:14,187
book about his voyage to pack a cannon.

291
00:11:14,187 --> 00:11:16,159
History has a nasty sense of humor.

292
00:11:16,159 --> 00:11:18,083
Most pirate voyages never

293
00:11:18,083 --> 00:11:19,481
needed those loaded guns.

294
00:11:19,481 --> 00:11:21,040
But when a real battle came,

295
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:23,568
it was the gunners who decided it.

296
00:11:23,568 --> 00:11:25,226
In November seventeen eighteen,

297
00:11:25,226 --> 00:11:26,801
at Ocracoke Inlet,

298
00:11:26,801 --> 00:11:30,746
Lieutenant Robert Maynard's sloops rowed toward Blackbeard's.

299
00:11:30,746 --> 00:11:32,665
Johnson writes that Blackbeard's men

300
00:11:32,665 --> 00:11:35,237
fired a broadside "charged with all manner

301
00:11:35,237 --> 00:11:37,561
of small shot," and that the lieutenant's

302
00:11:37,561 --> 00:11:40,361
sloop had twenty men killed and wounded.

303
00:11:40,361 --> 00:11:42,846
The Smithsonian puts it at twenty-one,

304
00:11:42,846 --> 00:11:43,723
in seconds.

305
00:11:43,723 --> 00:11:46,163
Both accounts say Blackbeard's

306
00:11:46,163 --> 00:11:47,413
crew then threw grenades,

307
00:11:47,413 --> 00:11:49,450
which Johnson describes as case bottles

308
00:11:49,450 --> 00:11:51,560
filled with powder and small shot.

309
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:53,056
Maynard had hidden most

310
00:11:53,056 --> 00:11:54,665
of his remaining men below.

311
00:11:54,665 --> 00:11:56,101
When Blackbeard boarded,

312
00:11:56,101 --> 00:11:57,306
they came up,

313
00:11:57,306 --> 00:12:00,142
and Blackbeard was killed in the fight that followed.

314
00:12:00,142 --> 00:12:02,855
Johnson's book alone lists Blackbeard's gunner,

315
00:12:02,855 --> 00:12:04,121
Philip Morton,

316
00:12:04,121 --> 00:12:06,017
among the pirates killed that day.

317
00:12:06,017 --> 00:12:07,729
Most of Blackbeard's surviving

318
00:12:07,729 --> 00:12:10,192
men were later tried and hanged in Virginia,

319
00:12:10,192 --> 00:12:11,537
as the South Carolina

320
00:12:11,537 --> 00:12:14,145
Encyclopedia and Johnson both record.

321
00:12:14,145 --> 00:12:17,200
The broadside had won the first minute.

322
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:18,671
It did not win the day.

323
00:12:18,671 --> 00:12:19,709
Three years later,

324
00:12:19,709 --> 00:12:23,101
off West Africa, gunnery decided another fight.

325
00:12:23,443 --> 00:12:26,053
Royal Museums Greenwich says that on

326
00:12:26,053 --> 00:12:26,900
the tenth of February,

327
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:29,707
seventeen twenty-two, a series of broadsides from Captain

328
00:12:29,707 --> 00:12:31,284
Chaloner Ogle's ship,

329
00:12:31,284 --> 00:12:35,790
the Swallow, left Bartholomew Roberts's ship nearly unable to move,

330
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:38,271
her masts and rigging shattered.

331
00:12:38,821 --> 00:12:40,365
Grapeshot from the Swallow,

332
00:12:40,365 --> 00:12:43,055
lead balls fired together to strike men

333
00:12:43,055 --> 00:12:45,520
rather than wood, killed Roberts at once.

334
00:12:46,053 --> 00:12:47,969
Johnson describes the same shot.

335
00:12:47,969 --> 00:12:50,210
His crew fought on for a time,

336
00:12:50,210 --> 00:12:51,481
and then surrendered.

337
00:12:51,481 --> 00:12:52,175
That is the

338
00:12:52,175 --> 00:12:54,543
uncomfortable math of your trade.

339
00:12:54,543 --> 00:12:56,681
Your guns were made to win without firing.

340
00:12:56,681 --> 00:12:58,157
When they did fire,

341
00:12:58,157 --> 00:13:01,452
they could win the first minute and still lose the fight,

342
00:13:01,452 --> 00:13:03,299
or meet a navy warship with

343
00:13:03,299 --> 00:13:05,250
far more guns than any pirate sloop.

344
00:13:05,767 --> 00:13:06,662
You think about that

345
00:13:06,662 --> 00:13:08,198
during a quiet week of rain.

346
00:13:08,635 --> 00:13:10,376
Rain is your real enemy.

347
00:13:10,376 --> 00:13:12,737
Damp powder will not fire,

348
00:13:12,737 --> 00:13:15,985
and a gun that will not fire is just a heavy bucket.

349
00:13:16,438 --> 00:13:18,889
So you spend the whole week opening barrels,

350
00:13:18,889 --> 00:13:20,680
spreading powder on sailcloth

351
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:23,792
under a tarpaulin whenever the sun comes out,

352
00:13:23,792 --> 00:13:26,021
and covering it again when it does not.

353
00:13:26,021 --> 00:13:27,645
The crew watches you guard

354
00:13:27,645 --> 00:13:29,643
black dust as if it were gold.

355
00:13:29,643 --> 00:13:31,039
In a way, it is.

356
00:13:31,477 --> 00:13:32,284
Without it,

357
00:13:32,284 --> 00:13:34,571
your two little guns are furniture.

358
00:13:34,571 --> 00:13:36,191
Then, one evening,

359
00:13:36,191 --> 00:13:38,846
a spark jumps from the galley fire and lands

360
00:13:38,846 --> 00:13:41,091
near a loose powder bag on the deck.

361
00:13:41,091 --> 00:13:42,190
Everyone freezes.

362
00:13:42,548 --> 00:13:44,742
Tom, who has never moved quickly in

363
00:13:44,742 --> 00:13:47,449
this entire series, steps on it with

364
00:13:47,449 --> 00:13:49,699
his boot before anyone else can breathe.

365
00:13:49,699 --> 00:13:51,027
You thank him.

366
00:13:51,027 --> 00:13:52,144
He says he has been

367
00:13:52,144 --> 00:13:53,595
practicing with his pipe.

368
00:13:53,595 --> 00:13:55,754
You decide not to ask what that means.

369
00:13:55,754 --> 00:13:57,090
The men who ran the guns

370
00:13:57,090 --> 00:13:58,669
did not do well in court.

371
00:13:58,669 --> 00:13:59,660
At Cape Coast,

372
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:03,009
Thomas Sutton, the gunner who had not taken half,

373
00:14:03,009 --> 00:14:05,662
was sentenced to death, and he was among the

374
00:14:05,662 --> 00:14:08,288
first six men hanged outside the castle.

375
00:14:08,288 --> 00:14:09,759
Johnson's book alone

376
00:14:09,759 --> 00:14:11,580
gives Sutton a last joke.

377
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:13,785
In irons, Sutton is said to have

378
00:14:13,785 --> 00:14:16,109
mocked a prisoner who prayed for heaven.

379
00:14:16,482 --> 00:14:17,705
Did he ever hear of

380
00:14:17,705 --> 00:14:19,845
pirates going there, Sutton asked.

381
00:14:19,845 --> 00:14:22,198
He would rather go to the other place,

382
00:14:22,198 --> 00:14:23,187
where it was merrier,

383
00:14:23,187 --> 00:14:25,436
and he would give Roberts a salute

384
00:14:25,436 --> 00:14:27,239
of thirteen guns when he arrived.

385
00:14:27,239 --> 00:14:29,468
Whether he said it or not,

386
00:14:29,468 --> 00:14:31,062
that is a gunner's joke.

387
00:14:31,062 --> 00:14:32,688
Even facing the gallows,

388
00:14:32,688 --> 00:14:35,110
he was still thinking in salutes.

389
00:14:35,949 --> 00:14:37,566
On John Phillips's crew,

390
00:14:37,566 --> 00:14:39,248
the gunner, James Sparks,

391
00:14:39,248 --> 00:14:42,351
did not survive the day the captives rose up

392
00:14:42,351 --> 00:14:43,656
in April seventeen twenty-four.

393
00:14:44,126 --> 00:14:45,826
The Boston News-Letter reported that

394
00:14:45,826 --> 00:14:47,991
the captives fell upon the gunner,

395
00:14:47,991 --> 00:14:50,665
and the pirates were overpowered within minutes.

396
00:14:50,665 --> 00:14:52,700
In all, fifty-two of Roberts's men

397
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:55,512
were hanged after the Cape Coast trial.

398
00:14:55,512 --> 00:14:57,065
More than seventy were acquitted,

399
00:14:57,065 --> 00:14:59,642
most of them men who had been forced aboard.

400
00:14:59,642 --> 00:15:00,852
The gunner from the

401
00:15:00,852 --> 00:15:03,095
Liverpool ship walked free.

402
00:15:03,095 --> 00:15:04,676
Thomas Sutton did not.

403
00:15:04,676 --> 00:15:07,203
Then the dealer brings the news

404
00:15:07,203 --> 00:15:08,538
you have heard before:

405
00:15:08,538 --> 00:15:10,391
the king's pardon of seventeen seventeen.

406
00:15:10,843 --> 00:15:13,396
Surrender to a governor by the fifth of September,

407
00:15:13,396 --> 00:15:14,142
seventeen eighteen,

408
00:15:14,142 --> 00:15:18,032
and earlier piracies would be forgiven.

409
00:15:18,032 --> 00:15:19,652
You read the reward list.

410
00:15:19,652 --> 00:15:22,113
As the London Gazette printed it,

411
00:15:22,113 --> 00:15:24,787
a pirate commander was worth one hundred pounds,

412
00:15:24,787 --> 00:15:26,606
and "every lieutenant,

413
00:15:26,606 --> 00:15:30,331
master, boatswain, carpenter, and gunner" forty.

414
00:15:30,331 --> 00:15:31,737
Your name is on it,

415
00:15:31,737 --> 00:15:34,423
beside the carpenter and the navigator.

416
00:15:34,423 --> 00:15:37,074
You came willingly, so you have a vote.

417
00:15:37,480 --> 00:15:39,207
You look at your two little guns,

418
00:15:39,207 --> 00:15:42,331
and at the warships the dealer says are coming.

419
00:15:42,331 --> 00:15:44,341
You raise your hand for the pardon.

420
00:15:44,341 --> 00:15:46,046
It wins, narrowly.

421
00:15:46,046 --> 00:15:48,484
The captain asks whether you will fire

422
00:15:48,484 --> 00:15:51,289
a salute as they sail in to surrender.

423
00:15:51,289 --> 00:15:51,986
You say no.

424
00:15:51,986 --> 00:15:53,679
You have a better idea.

425
00:15:54,261 --> 00:15:56,212
That evening, you draw the charges

426
00:15:56,212 --> 00:15:58,015
from both guns: the wad,

427
00:15:58,015 --> 00:16:02,164
the shot, the powder, one careful piece at a time,

428
00:16:02,164 --> 00:16:04,294
the way the conservators would do it three

429
00:16:04,294 --> 00:16:06,998
hundred years later on Blackbeard's cannons.

430
00:16:06,998 --> 00:16:08,310
You lay it all out on

431
00:16:08,310 --> 00:16:09,742
the deck in a neat row.

432
00:16:09,742 --> 00:16:11,625
An unloaded gun cannot

433
00:16:11,625 --> 00:16:13,322
start anything by accident.

434
00:16:13,322 --> 00:16:15,030
Neither, you hope, can you.

435
00:16:15,030 --> 00:16:17,156
Tom watches the whole thing,

436
00:16:17,156 --> 00:16:20,620
sponge in hand, and asks what happens to the guns now.

437
00:16:20,620 --> 00:16:22,385
You tell him they will probably

438
00:16:22,385 --> 00:16:23,985
end up on somebody else's ship,

439
00:16:23,985 --> 00:16:26,292
aimed at somebody else's problems.

440
00:16:26,292 --> 00:16:28,242
He asks if he can keep the sponge.

441
00:16:28,242 --> 00:16:28,967
You let him.

442
00:16:28,967 --> 00:16:30,446
He has earned it.

443
00:16:30,446 --> 00:16:31,740
So why was being a pirate

444
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:33,184
gunner worse than you think?

445
00:16:33,184 --> 00:16:34,794
Because your best day

446
00:16:34,794 --> 00:16:36,349
was the day you never fired.

447
00:16:36,349 --> 00:16:37,904
Because you slept on the one

448
00:16:37,904 --> 00:16:40,588
cargo that could kill everyone aboard.

449
00:16:40,588 --> 00:16:42,467
Because when a real battle came,

450
00:16:42,467 --> 00:16:44,053
your guns could win the first

451
00:16:44,053 --> 00:16:46,438
minute and still lose you everything.

452
00:16:46,438 --> 00:16:47,695
And because every gun you

453
00:16:47,695 --> 00:16:49,243
aimed was aimed at people whose

454
00:16:49,243 --> 00:16:51,011
only crime was being in the way.

455
00:16:51,011 --> 00:16:52,274
A court did not care

456
00:16:52,274 --> 00:16:54,287
how good a shot you were.

457
00:16:54,287 --> 00:16:55,919
It cared who you had aimed at.

458
00:16:55,919 --> 00:16:58,640
If you want another life behind the legends,

459
00:16:58,640 --> 00:16:59,721
and another job that

460
00:16:59,721 --> 00:17:02,095
sounds better than it was, subscribe.

461
00:17:02,095 --> 00:17:03,305
This is Pirate Pages.

462
00:17:03,305 --> 00:17:05,354
The lives behind the legends.
